> The agentic lane is **38.4% supervised by token**: of 11,781,899 tokens only 4,529,722 carry loss. The rest are tool schemas, user turns and observation slots that the model reads but must never be trained to reproduce. A lane sized on raw dataset bytes would have over-counted this lane by 2.6x.

## Anneal reserve: how much actually qualifies

The admission criteria in `mixture/v5_mixture.json` applied to real documents.

| lane | clean tokens | reserve-admitted tokens | fill rate |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| agentic | 11,781,899 | 75,996 | 0.6% |
| code | 17,412,250 | 5,223,432 | 30.0% |
| general_web | 3,506,900 | 3,003,960 | 85.7% |
| indic | 13,643,684 | 10,745,464 | 78.8% |
| long_context | 28,586,780 | 27,667,625 | 96.8% |
| reasoning | 5,012,873 | 0 | 0.0% |
| stem_math | 1,097,781 | 0 | 0.0% |
| **total** | **81,042,167** | **46,716,477** | **57.6%** |

What those fill rates imply for the declared reserve, lane by lane:

| lane | reserve demand | measured fill rate | cleaned pool needed to fill it |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| agentic | 10B | 0.6% | 1.55T |
| code | 18B | 30.0% | 0.06T |
| general_web | 6B | 85.7% | 0.01T |
| indic | 22B | 78.8% | 0.03T |
| long_context | 12B | 96.8% | 0.01T |
| reasoning | 18B | 0.0% | **unreachable at this fill rate** |
| stem_math | 14B | 0.0% | **unreachable at this fill rate** |

> This is the whole argument for the reserve in one table. The lanes fed by curated sources fill easily - a public-domain book *is* long-context reserve material, so that fill rate is high by construction. The two lanes the model is actually being built for do not fill: at the measured 0.6% agentic fill rate, a 10B agentic reserve would need ~1.7T of cleaned agentic-lane candidates, and no such pool exists (total real supply is 0.63B). At the measured 0% reasoning fill rate it cannot be filled at all from open data. Both have to be manufactured deliberately, which is exactly why the reserve is a Session-5 allocation decision and not something discovered at the end of the run.

